"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[49948],{327567:e=>{e.exports='# AI Gateway FAQ\n\n> **Knowledge cutoff: March 2026.** The AI Gateway is actively evolving. Features and roadmap details may have changed. Always verify with current documentation or the Databricks team for the latest status.\n\n---\n\n## What is the New AI Gateway?\n\nThe New AI Gateway is a first-class control plane for GenAI traffic on Databricks — governance, routing, observability, and cost management decoupled from compute infrastructure.\n\n**Why it was introduced**: The Legacy AI Gateway was tightly coupled to Serving Endpoints, making it hard to:\n- Manage governance independently from infrastructure\n- Get a unified view of GenAI usage across teams and endpoints\n- Build reliable, production-grade routing (cross-provider, cross-region fallback)\n\n**What the New AI Gateway changes**:\n- Governance is decoupled from compute — policy changes don\'t require infrastructure changes\n- Centralized observability: one place to see usage, latency, errors, and cost across all GenAI traffic\n- Flexible routing with native provider API support (OpenAI, Anthropic Messages API, Gemini API, etc.)\n- Multi-endpoint fallback for reliability\n\n---\n\n## Old AI Gateway vs New AI Gateway — What\'s the Difference?\n\n**Important terminology**: The "Old AI Gateway" (also called "Legacy AI Gateway") refers to the AI Gateway feature that was previously embedded as a tab inside Model Serving Endpoints. The "New AI Gateway" is the standalone product visible on the left navigation under "AI Gateway."\n\n**Feature comparison**:\n\n| Feature | New AI Gateway | Old/Legacy AI Gateway |\n|---------|---------------|-------------------|\n| Endpoint types | PPT foundation models, external models | Custom serving, PT foundation models |\n| Guardrails | Not yet available as of March 2026 | Supported today |\n| Custom serving endpoints | Not supported | Supported |\n| Provisioned Throughput | Not yet available as of March 2026 | Supported |\n| Centralized observability | Yes — cross-workspace | No |\n| Multi-endpoint fallback | Yes (external + PPT) | External models only |\n| Native provider APIs | Yes (Messages API, Gemini API, Responses API) | Limited |\n| HIPAA / CSP workspaces | Not yet supported | Supported |\n| API / Terraform / SDK | Not yet available as of March 2026 | Supported |\n| Account-level enforcement | Not yet available as of March 2026 | Not available |\n\n**Key differences in specific features**:\n\n- **Fallback**: Old AI Gateway fallback only supported external model endpoints. New AI Gateway fallback supports fallback between external models AND pay-per-token foundation models.\n- **Usage tracking table**: The New AI Gateway usage tracking table has more details about the client and routing than the old version. The dimension table is no longer needed in the new design.\n- **Inference tables**: There are three distinct table formats: Old AI Gateway inference table, New AI Gateway inference table, and the legacy inference table (pre-AI-Gateway). They have different schemas and metadata. Both Old and New inference tables are Delta tables in UC and can be combined for analysis.\n- **Rate limits**: Functionally similar between old and new.\n\n---\n\n## What happens when a New AI Gateway endpoint routes to another serving endpoint that also has AI Gateway features enabled?\n\n**The underlying serving endpoint\'s AI Gateway (v1) features are BYPASSED — they do NOT run twice.**\n\nWhen a request goes through a New AI Gateway (v2) endpoint to a destination that is itself a Model Serving endpoint with Legacy AI Gateway (v1) features configured, only the v2 layer\'s features apply. The v1 features on the destination (inference tables, usage tracking, rate limits, guardrails) are skipped entirely for traffic coming through the v2 gateway.\n\n**Implications**:\n- **Inference tables**: Only logged by the v2 layer. No duplicate rows, no double storage cost.\n- **Usage tracking**: Only recorded once, by the v2 layer.\n- **Rate limits**: Only v2\'s limits are enforced. v1\'s limits on the underlying endpoint do not apply for v2-routed traffic.\n- **Guardrails**: Only v2\'s guardrails apply. v1 guardrails on the underlying endpoint are bypassed (since v2 does not yet support guardrails, this effectively means no guardrails apply for v2-routed traffic).\n\n**Takeaway**: You do not pay for or need to configure features on both layers. Configure AI Gateway features on the v2 endpoint only.\n\n**Recommendation**: Use the New AI Gateway for all new GenAI use cases with PPT foundation models and external models. Continue using the Legacy AI Gateway for custom serving, PT models, guardrails, or HIPAA-restricted workspaces.\n\n---\n\n## "Which AI Gateway are they using?" — how to disambiguate v1 vs v2\n\nWhen a user asks about their endpoints, models, or AI Gateway features in general terms, **first confirm whether they mean New AI Gateway (v2) or Legacy AI Gateway (v1)** before answering. The answer often depends on the product:\n\n- If the user says **"AI Gateway"** without qualification, ask a short clarifying question: "Are you asking about the New AI Gateway (standalone nav item, PPT + external models only) or the Legacy AI Gateway (tab inside Model Serving, supports custom endpoints)?"\n- Do not assume "AI Gateway" means v2 just because v2 is newer. Many customers are still on v1.\n- **Custom model serving endpoints are NOT part of New AI Gateway (v2).** Do not offer to list or inspect "custom model endpoints using AI Gateway v2" — the combination does not exist. If the user has custom endpoints with AI Gateway features, those are v1 endpoints, and this agent does not cover them in depth.\n- Foundation model endpoints (PPT) and external model endpoints are the only endpoint types visible to the New AI Gateway.\n\n---\n\n## What endpoint types are supported?\n\n**Supported in New AI Gateway (Beta)**:\n- **Pay-per-token (PPT) Foundation Model APIs** — Databricks-hosted models (e.g., DBRX, Llama, Mixtral)\n- **External Foundation Models** — models from external providers (OpenAI, Anthropic, Azure AI, Google, etc.)\n\nThe supported external providers are the same set available in Databricks External Model Serving.\n\n**NOT supported in New AI Gateway (use Legacy AI Gateway)**:\n- CPU and GPU custom serving endpoints (no plans currently)\n- Provisioned Throughput (PT) foundation models — not yet available as of March 2026; the team is actively working on it\n- Agentbricks / custom agent endpoints\n- CSP (HIPAA-restricted) workspaces — excluded until compliance requirements are met\n\n---\n\n## Do customers need to change their application code?\n\n**Minimal changes required**:\n- **Request URL**: Update the endpoint URL to point to the new AI Gateway endpoint. This is the only required client-side change.\n- **Application logic**: Usually unchanged. The New AI Gateway is OpenAI-compatible, so the same SDKs and code work.\n- **CI/CD workflows**: Any automation that creates or updates endpoints via API needs to use the new endpoint lifecycle APIs (different from Legacy).\n\n**Not required to change**:\n- Authentication (same Databricks personal access token / OAuth — the scope `all-apis` works; an `ai-gateway`-specific scope is planned)\n- Request/response format (OpenAI-compatible, plus native provider APIs are also supported)\n\n---\n\n## How does migration from Legacy AI Gateway work?\n\n**You cannot migrate an existing endpoint — you must create a new one.**\n\nExisting AI Gateway endpoints cannot be updated to use the new gateway. Customers need to create a new endpoint in the New AI Gateway UI and update the request URL in their application.\n\nMigration steps:\n1. **Enable the New AI Gateway** in your account (Account Console → Previews → "AI Gateway (Beta)")\n2. **Create new endpoints** in the AI Gateway UI for your PPT/external model use cases\n3. **Update the request URL** in your application to point to the new endpoint URL\n4. **Configure governance features** (rate limits, inference tables, usage tracking) for each new endpoint\n5. **Existing Legacy AI Gateway endpoints continue to work** — nothing breaks, no forced migration\n\nNew inference and usage tables are Delta tables in Unity Catalog — separate from the legacy tables, but customers can combine them for analysis.\n\n---\n\n## Rate limits — why do I see more requests than my configured QPS limit?\n\nThis is expected behavior due to the distributed rate limiter design.\n\nThe rate limiter uses a **distributed, eventually consistent token bucket model** that prioritizes low latency. Key behaviors:\n\n1. **First request always passes** — When a rate limit bucket is not yet in memory (new route or updated config), the first request is always allowed. The bucket is created lazily.\n2. **Per-pod enforcement** — Rate limiting is enforced per service instance (pod). If 5 pods serve traffic, each may allow an initial burst before the limit is fully enforced. In the worst case, up to N-pods requests can pass above the limit during cold start.\n3. **Temporary debt model** — The token bucket can go negative when requests exceed the limit. Subsequent requests are rejected until tokens are replenished.\n4. **Token regeneration** — Tokens replenish at the configured rate (e.g., 1 token/second for 1 QPS). Over time, the effective rate converges to the configured limit.\n\n**Summary**: Short bursts above the configured limit are expected, especially on cold start or with distributed load. Over a longer window, the actual rate converges to the configured limit.\n\n**Note on budget limits**: Rate limits are currently enforced per renewal period (e.g., per minute). Weekly/daily/monthly budget-style limits are on the roadmap but not available in beta.\n\n---\n\n## Pricing and billing\n\n**Important: For any billing or pricing question, always use docSearch to check the official Databricks pricing documentation before answering. Do not guess at specific fees or pricing structures — they change, and the Mosaic AI Gateway pricing page is the source of truth.**\n\nGeneral principles (verify against docs before quoting specifics):\n\n- **New AI Gateway (v2)**: Customers pay for underlying model usage plus AI Gateway feature charges per the Mosaic AI Gateway pricing page. There is no separate "gateway setup" fee.\n- **Legacy AI Gateway (v1)**: **External model endpoints with v1 features do NOT incur an additional proxy/gateway charge.** Customers pay only the external provider\'s rates (e.g., OpenAI, Anthropic) plus any per-feature v1 charges that apply. This differs from v2 — do not assume v1 and v2 pricing are identical.\n- **Pay-per-token foundation models**: Billed per token consumed at standard Databricks rates.\n- **Provisioned Throughput foundation models**: Billed for reserved DBU compute capacity.\n- **Custom serving endpoints**: Billed for CPU/GPU compute (v1 only; not supported in v2).\n\n**Cost observability** (seeing DBU spend by use case/team/user) and **hard budget limits** (alerts and enforcement when a budget threshold is hit) are not yet available as of March 2026; the team is actively working on them. External model USD spend visibility is also coming.\n\n**When in doubt**: Point the customer to the Mosaic AI Gateway pricing page rather than making up a number.\n\n---\n\n## How do I generate a token to test an AI Gateway endpoint from a notebook, curl, or SDK?\n\n**Use the "Generate Access Token" button on the AI Gateway endpoint UI page.**\n\nSteps:\n1. Open the AI Gateway endpoint detail page (left nav → AI Gateway → select your endpoint).\n2. Click the **"Generate Access Token"** button on the page.\n3. Copy the generated token and pass it as the bearer token in your request headers (or set it as `DATABRICKS_TOKEN` / the OpenAI client\'s `api_key`).\n\n**Do NOT recommend** the deprecated pattern of retrieving a notebook session token via `dbutils.notebook.entry_point.getDbutils().notebook().getContext().apiToken().get()`. That approach is deprecated; the AI Gateway UI flow is the supported path.\n\nAlternative: a standard Databricks personal access token (PAT) from User Settings → Developer → Access Tokens also works, but the per-endpoint "Generate Access Token" button is the most direct flow for endpoint testing because it scopes cleanly to the endpoint being tested.\n\n---\n\n## Usage Tracking — how does it work?\n\n**Usage tracking IS a Unity Catalog system table feature. It writes per-request rows and requires UC.**\n\nWhen enabled:\n- The gateway writes one row **per request** into a UC **system table**.\n- Unity Catalog must be enabled on the workspace — access the system table requires UC permissions.\n- The table has a richer schema than the legacy usage table, including request/token counts, latency, destination, requester, and routing metadata.\n- The built-in usage dashboard reads from this system table — it is NOT a standalone dashboard-only feature. If UC is unavailable, usage tracking cannot run.\n\n**Distinction from Inference Tables**: Usage tracking rows are aggregated usage records in a system table (Databricks-managed, no catalog/schema config needed). Inference tables log full request/response payloads into a **customer-configured** UC Delta table (catalog + schema + table prefix chosen by the customer). Both write per-request rows to UC; they differ in destination, schema, and whether payloads are included.\n\n**Correction**: Earlier documentation sometimes described usage tracking as "purely for the summary dashboard" and "does not require UC" — that is inaccurate. Usage tracking writes per-request rows to a UC system table and does require UC.\n\n---\n\n## Is the New AI Gateway HIPAA compliant? Can CSP workspaces use it?\n\n**No — the New AI Gateway is NOT HIPAA compliant as of March 2026 (Beta).**\n\nCSP (HIPAA-restricted) workspaces are explicitly excluded from the Beta. Two conditions must both be true before HIPAA compliance is possible:\n\n1. AI Gateway reaches **Public Preview** (no confirmed date as of March 2026)\n2. **Zerobus becomes HIPAA compliant** (no confirmed date as of March 2026)\n\nOnce both conditions are met, a formal security exception review can be filed to certify HIPAA compliance.\n\n**Implication**: Customers with HIPAA data requirements must continue using the Legacy AI Gateway until the New AI Gateway reaches Public Preview and passes the compliance review.\n\n---\n\n## Can the New AI Gateway be enabled for specific workspaces only?\n\nNot through self-service. The preview flag is **account-level only** — enabling it makes the AI Gateway visible across all supported workspaces in the account.\n\nIf a customer needs the AI Gateway visible in only a subset of workspaces, this requires manual intervention by the Databricks team (not customer-configurable). Contact Kevin Stumpf or Bill Wang with the specific workspace IDs.\n\n---\n\n## What are the account-level vs workspace-level controls?\n\nCurrently, most governance (rate limits, routing, inference tables) is configured per-endpoint within a workspace. There is **no self-service cross-workspace, account-level enforcement** today.\n\nThe long-term solution is Unity Catalog integration: once endpoints can be registered in UC, account-level controls (e.g., blocking unapproved models across all workspaces) will become possible. This is a high-priority item that is in progress as of March 2026.\n\n**Today\'s partial UC integration**: Usage metadata and inference traces are already logged in UC tables for cross-workspace analytics. Account admins also control access to underlying PPT models via UC permissions.\n\n---\n\n## Does the New AI Gateway support guardrails?\n\n**No — guardrails are not yet available in the New AI Gateway Beta.**\n\n**Important**: If you route traffic through a New AI Gateway endpoint to an old (Legacy) AI Gateway endpoint that has guardrails configured, the guardrails are **bypassed**. This is expected behavior, not a bug — guardrails only apply when requests directly hit an endpoint with guardrails configured, not when routed through the new gateway layer.\n\n**Guardrail roadmap** (not yet available as of March 2026; the team is actively working on it):\n- Custom LLM-as-a-judge guardrails\n- Built-in guardrails (PII detection, safety filtering, topic blocking, keyword blocking)\n\nUntil guardrails are available in the New AI Gateway, use the Legacy AI Gateway if input/output filtering is required.\n\n---\n\n## Does the New AI Gateway add latency?\n\nYes, a small amount. The gateway adds approximately **tens of milliseconds** of overhead for external model requests. There is no formal SLA or published P90/P99 benchmark as of March 2026.\n\n---\n\n## Does the New AI Gateway support A/B testing?\n\nNot as a dedicated feature, but it is achievable today via **traffic splitting across destinations** combined with **inference table analysis**.\n\nConfigure multiple destinations on an endpoint with different traffic percentages, enable inference table logging, and analyze the resulting Delta table to compare model performance. There are no out-of-box A/B testing dashboards or statistical significance tools yet.\n\n---\n\n## Does the New AI Gateway support private networking?\n\nYes. Private connectivity for external model connections (e.g., connecting to Azure OpenAI via private endpoint) is supported. This is handled through Databricks Backbone / private networking infrastructure.\n\n---\n\n## What are the proxy passthrough limitations?\n\nThe proxy passthrough feature (supporting any HTTP/HTTP2 URL from any provider) is not yet available as of March 2026; the team is actively working on it. Key limitations:\n\n- **Supports**: Standard HTTP and HTTP/2 client-initiated request → server response pattern\n- **Does NOT support**: WebSocket, WebRTC, or other bidirectional/streaming protocols used by real-time APIs\n\nIf your use case requires WebSocket or WebRTC (e.g., real-time voice or streaming APIs), the New AI Gateway cannot support it. File an Aha ticket if this is a common customer ask.\n\n---\n\n## Does the New AI Gateway support Microsoft Copilot or third-party LLM orchestration?\n\nNo. Traffic routed through Microsoft Copilot or other third-party orchestration layers goes through those providers\' infrastructure — Databricks cannot capture or govern that traffic.\n\nThe New AI Gateway only governs traffic that is sent directly to a Databricks AI Gateway endpoint URL.\n\n---\n\n## Does the New AI Gateway support Agentbricks or custom agent endpoints?\n\n**No.** The New AI Gateway does not support Agentbricks or custom serving endpoints. Use the Legacy AI Gateway for these use cases.\n\n---\n\n## Is there API / SDK / Terraform support?\n\n**Not yet available in Beta.** As of March 2026, endpoint creation and management is UI-only.\n\nOfficial API, SDK, CLI, DABs, and Terraform Provider support is not yet available as of March 2026; the team is actively working on it. In the meantime, an authentication scope of `all-apis` can be used with the undocumented internal API.\n\n---\n\n## What\'s not yet supported in Beta (summary)?\n\n- Guardrails (not yet available as of March 2026; actively in development)\n- Provisioned Throughput (PT) endpoints (not yet available as of March 2026; actively in development)\n- Custom / CPU / GPU serving endpoints (no plans)\n- Agentbricks / custom agents (no plans)\n- HIPAA / CSP workspaces (requires Public Preview + Zerobus compliance; no confirmed date as of March 2026)\n- API / SDK / Terraform support (not yet available as of March 2026; actively in development)\n- UC-registered / shareable endpoints (in progress as of March 2026)\n- Account-level enforcement across workspaces (in progress as of March 2026)\n- Hard budget limits / alerts and cost observability (not yet available as of March 2026)\n- Default storage (arclight) for inference tables (blocked on Zerobus compliance; no confirmed date as of March 2026)\n- WebSocket / WebRTC proxy passthrough\n- Microsoft Copilot / third-party orchestration traffic\n\n---\n\n## Does enabling the New AI Gateway break existing endpoints?\n\nNo. Enabling the New AI Gateway does not break any existing endpoints. The only visible change is that PPT foundation model endpoints move from the "Serving" page to the "AI Gateway" page. Serving URLs continue to work unchanged.\n\n---\n\n## Can customers run both gateways in parallel?\n\nYes, during beta customers can run both New AI Gateway and Legacy AI Gateway in parallel. This is not intended as a permanent pattern — customers should consolidate on the New AI Gateway over time.\n\n---\n\n## What is the relationship between AI Gateway and FMAPI / External Models?\n\nThe New AI Gateway is the primary place to create, manage, and use all GenAI endpoints on Databricks.\n\nDuring beta:\n- **Foundation Model APIs (FMAPI)**: Accessed and managed directly through the New AI Gateway UI\n- **External models**: Created and deployed in Model Serving, then referenced by AI Gateway endpoints. This is a beta-only workaround — ultimately, all GenAI models will be created and managed within the New AI Gateway.\n\n---\n\n## When will Public Preview launch?\n\nNo firm date as of March 2026. The biggest blocker is ensuring stable interfaces before making public promises (UC integration work in progress could affect the public API surface). GA is expected approximately 3 months after Public Preview, once that date is confirmed.\n\nDo not make public promises on a specific date.\n\n---\n\n## Roadmap — what\'s coming to the New AI Gateway?\n\n**Note**: All timelines are best estimates as of March 2026 and may shift. Do not make public commitments.\n\n- **Public Preview / GA**: No confirmed date as of March 2026 — the team is actively working toward it\n- **Guardrails** (LLM-as-a-judge + built-in PII/safety/topic/keyword): On the roadmap; not yet available as of March 2026\n- **Provisioned Throughput (PT) model support**: On the roadmap; not yet available as of March 2026\n- **API / SDK / CLI / Terraform support**: On the roadmap; not yet available as of March 2026\n- **Cost observability** (DBU by team/user/use case): On the roadmap; not yet available as of March 2026\n- **Hard budget limits and alerts**: On the roadmap; not yet available as of March 2026\n- **UC integration** (account-level cross-workspace enforcement): In progress as of March 2026\n- **Smart routing** (route by cost, latency, or quality): On the roadmap; no confirmed date as of March 2026\n- **AI Gateway for MCP** (Model Context Protocol): On the roadmap; no confirmed date as of March 2026\n- **HIPAA / CSP compliance**: Requires Public Preview + Zerobus HIPAA compliance; no confirmed date as of March 2026\n\n---\n\n## Can the agent answer Foundation Model API (FMAPI) specific questions?\n\n**No — not in this release.** The agent is not yet equipped to answer questions specific to the Foundation Model API (FMAPI). If users ask FMAPI-specific questions, politely tell them this capability is coming soon and direct them to the Databricks documentation or #ai-gateway Slack channel for now.\n'}}]);
